{"data":{"updated_timestamp":"31/10/2026 20:07:41","profile":{"player_id":"63604","last_updated":"31/10/2026 20:07","fullname":"David Guy Bedingham","short_display_name":"D Bedingham","name":"David Bedingham","gender":"M","dob":"22/04/1994","date_of_death":null,"place_birth":"South Africa","nationality_id":"7","nationality":"South Africa","nationality_short_code":"SA","nick_name":null,"player_skill":{"id":"1","text":"Batsman","role":"Batter"},"batting_style":{"id":"1","text":"Right Handed"},"bowling_style":{"id":"","text":"-"},"writeup":"<p>David Bedingham has never lacked for strokes, but it is his remarkable resilience that gives his story its defining shape. An aggressive and technically polished right-handed middle-order batter, he has built a stellar reputation on heavy scoring across formats.&nbsp;Born on April 22, 1994, in George, South Africa, Bedingham emerged as a technically fluent and explosive right-handed middle-order batter. His early exploits showcased not only a natural flair for elegant stroke play but also an immense composure under pressure - a rare quality that separates good players from great ones.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>Growing up through Wynberg Boys' High School and the Western Province age-group systems, Bedingham's early promise earned him selection for the South Africa Under-19 team. However, a major car accident in 2016 left him sidelined with severe fractures, keeping him away from professional cricket for nearly a year and casting profound doubt on his future in the sport.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>Demonstrating immense grit, he returned stronger than ever. Turning out for KwaZulu-Natal and Western Province, he rebuilt his domestic career and evolved into one of the country's most prolific red-ball run-scorers. That relentless domestic form opened the door to English county cricket, where he signed with Durham in 2020.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>He quickly became an indispensable pillar of the team, accumulating thousands of runs and multiple centuries. His finest English summer arrived in 2024, when he finished as County Championship Division One&rsquo;s leading run-scorer with 1,331 runs across 11 matches, a campaign highlighted by a magnificent career-best score of 279 against Lancashire that broke the county's individual record.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>International recognition soon followed. Bedingham made his Test debut for South Africa against India at Centurion on Boxing Day 2023, immediately announcing himself with a fluent 56 in his first innings. He quickly cemented his place in the Proteas' middle order, bringing up his maiden Test century in February 2024 - a sublime 110 against New Zealand in Hamilton.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>His ability to counter both high-pace and spin with a healthy strike rate provided South Africa's batting lineup with vital stability and firepower, earning him a central contract as a core red-ball fixture. He later achieved the ultimate pinnacle of the format by anchoring the Proteas to historic glory in the 2025 ICC World Test Championship Final against Australia.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>Beyond the multi-day arena, Bedingham has showcased his boundary-hitting prowess in white-ball cricket. He has featured in the SA20 for Sunrisers Eastern Cape and Durban's Super Giants, while continuing to serve as a match-winner for Durham in the T20 Blast and One-Day Cup, alongside short-format stints in competitions like The Hundred. Moving between patient, big-scoring first-class innings and full-blooded short-format batting is a testament to his elite adaptability.</p>\r\n<br />\r\n<p>Following brilliant domestic form. highlighted by twin hundreds for Western Province at Newlands, he was recalled and picked in September 2026 for South Africa's home Test series against Australia.</p>","relation":null,"social_details":{"social_platform":[{"social_platform_id":"1","social_platform_name":"facebook","social_platform_url":""},{"social_platform_id":"2","social_platform_name":"twitter","social_platform_u